<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4965"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 8:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/10745103.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/10745103.htm</a> <BR>Posted on Thu, Jan. 27, 2005  <BR>POLICE TO TALK TODAY ABOUT CASE <BR>More BTK victims?  <BR> <BR>A possible clue found near Valley Center focuses speculation on 2 unsolved homicides in areas north of Wichita <BR> <BR>BY TIM POTTER AND HURST LAVIANA <BR> <BR>The Wichita Eagle <BR>================================================== <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/10745102.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/10745102.htm</a> <BR>Posted on Thu, Jan. 27, 2005 <BR>POSSIBLE BTK CONNECTIONS <BR>April 27, 1985 -- Park City resident Marine Hedge is abducted. Her car is found May 2 at 21st and Woodlawn; her purse is found May 5 near 143rd East and 37th North; her body is found May 5, 1985, near 53rd North and Webb Road. <BR> <BR>Jan. 19, 1991 -- Delores Davis abducted from her home one-half mile east of Park City. Her body is found Feb. 1, 1991, near 117th North and Meridian.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4965-->
<!--Post: 4972--><!--Time: 1106843063-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4972"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 8:24 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The short sightedness of the police: they overlook the obvious while looking for specific facts. As one great philosopher stated &#34;Not everything that is red is ketchup&#34;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4972-->
<!--Post: 4985--><!--Time: 1106844321-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4985"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Devbost<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Devbost<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 8:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It would certainly seem on the face of it that they previously ruled out a BTK connection based on the differences in the MO rather than the similarities, which are striking. <BR> <BR>Still, since they are being so tight-lipped about information on the case, there probably are valid reasons unknown to us why they had ruled out BTK to begin with. The acquisition of new evidence sometimes lead investigators to reevaluate previous conclusions. It looks like that&#39;s what&#39;s happening here. I&#39;m not sure an accusation of &#34;short-sightedness&#34; is warranted.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4985-->
<!--Post: 4990--><!--Time: 1106845180-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4990"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 8:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree.  Sometimes I think police are regarding their boundaries as officers and that in itself keeps them from seeing the obvious.  They have rules to follow...not their fault really. <BR> <BR>But when it comes to catching an elusive killer, especially one as elusive as BTK, I think there is permission to be a little more conspiratoral and consider all angles as to what some of his messages might mean.   <BR> <BR>Devbost, you make a good point about &#39;tight-lippedness&#39;being an obstacle...in one way it is a safe guard to an investigation, but then again, what we don&#39;t know can keep us from helping ... <BR> <BR>I really think the police are probably doing the best they can.  I think if I were in their positions, having acess to all facts in the case, yet having the responsibility of abiding by procedure and organization to follow up on an investigation, I think I might seem a little short-sighted too.  But...I am not the one doing the legwork, the paperwork and the mindwork on this case.  <BR> <BR>I can&#39;t blame the cops for BTK&#39;s elusiveness..but I can blame BTK for creating his own rules as to how he will be caught.  I can&#39;t imagine a killer with that kind of ability. Unless he is trained...WELL trained in fooling people for most of his life.   <BR> <BR>Kind of like the kid who gets away with lying, cheating and hurting because he was...Never CAUGHT.  <BR> <BR>JMO.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4990-->
